<p>Thermoplastic hydraulic hoses come in several versions, including the R7 and the R8. The difference lies in the pressure class: SAE 100R7 is the standard thermoplastic hydraulic hose and SAE 100R8 is the high-pressure version within the same family of materials. Both are available in black (conductive) and orange (non-conductive).</p>
<h2>What is an R7 hose and what is an R8 hose?</h2>
<p>Like rubber hydraulic hoses, thermoplastic hydraulic hoses fall under the SAE 100R classification. This classification groups hoses by construction and pressure class, so the designation alone indicates which category a hose belongs to. Within that series, R7 and R8 are listed separately:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>SAE 100R7</strong>: thermoplastic hydraulic hose, available in black (conductive) or orange (non-conductive).</li>
<li><strong>SAE 100R8:</strong> high-pressure thermoplastic hydraulic hose, also available in black (conductive) or orange (non-conductive).</li>
</ul>
<p>Both types consist of a thermoplastic elastomer inner tube, a reinforcement of polyester or aramid fibre and a polyurethane (PU) outer cover. This construction makes the hose lighter than a rubber equivalent, insensitive to corrosion, and recognisable by its smooth plastic cover, unlike the woven texture of a rubber hose.</p>
<p>Thermoplastic hoses are often chosen for systems where cleanliness and corrosion resistance are crucial, for example in a marine environment with exposure to UV radiation and salt water.</p>
<p>The distinction between R7 and R8 lies in the pressure class the hose is built for: R8 is the high-pressure version. To reach that higher pressure level, manufacturers usually use a heavier reinforcement around the polyester core, for example an additional steel or aramid layer. The exact number of layers and the reinforcement used differ per manufacturer and part number, and are stated in the specification of the selected item. As with any high-pressure hose, working pressure, burst pressure and bend radii depend on diameter, wall thickness and construction.</p>
<p>{{img:kbi-35998-0}}</p>
<h2>What to look for when choosing between R7 and R8?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>System working pressure:</strong> check the working pressure plus the required safety margin against the specification of the item. An R8 designation alone does not guarantee that a specific item can handle the required pressure.</li>
<li><strong>Medium and conductivity:</strong> choose the black, conductive version where there is a risk of electrostatic charging, for example when transporting fuel. Choose orange when that risk does not apply.</li>
<li><strong>Diameter and wall thickness:</strong> a larger hose diameter usually lowers the permitted working pressure within the same series. Check this per size, not on the basis of the R class alone.</li>
<li><strong>Ambient conditions:</strong> temperature fluctuations in the medium and the environment affect the strength of the plastic core and must be checked against the specification of the selected item.</li>
<li><strong>Couplings:</strong> the connection type must suit both the hose size and the system requirements; couplings from different product lines are not automatically interchangeable.</li>
<li><strong>Certification:</strong> determine in advance whether a certificate is required, as not every item within R7 or R8 carries the same certification.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Common mistakes and misconceptions about R7 and R8 hoses</h2>
<h3>Confusing class and item</h3>
<p>The R7 or R8 designation says something about the pressure class within the thermoplastic family, but not about the exact technical values of a specific hose. Those are listed in the product specification of the part number.</p>
<h3>Interpreting colour coding as pressure class</h3>
<p>The black and orange versions indicate whether a hose is conductive or non-conductive, not whether it is an R7 or R8 hose. Both classes are available in both colours.</p>
<h3>Replacing without reassessment</h3>
<p>Replacing a hose from one class with a hose from the other class without reassessing the working pressure, medium and ambient conditions undermines the safety margin of the system.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions about thermoplastic hydraulic hoses</h2>
<h3>1. Can a worn R7 hose simply be replaced by an R8 hose?</h3>
<p>In practice this happens often, because an R8 hose seems "better anyway". That is not necessarily true: R8 is designed for a higher pressure class, but that says nothing about the diameter, bend radius or coupling type required at that specific point in the installation. Replace on the basis of the system requirements, not on the letter R7 or R8 alone.</p>
<h3>2. Why does one thermoplastic hose have a black cover and the other an orange one?</h3>
<p>That is not about the pressure class, but about conductivity. Black is the conductive version and is used where electrostatic charging is a risk, such as when transporting fuel. Orange is the non-conductive version. Both colours are available in R7 as well as R8.</p>
<h3>3. Does the R designation automatically determine how much pressure a hose can handle?</h3>
<p>No. The designation indicates the class within the SAE 100R series, but the exact working pressure, burst pressure and bend radius depend on the diameter, wall thickness and construction of the specific item.</p>
