<p>In practice there is sometimes a tendency to use maintenance schedules in which hoses are renewed purely on the basis of the calendar. From a technical and financial point of view, this is rarely the most efficient method.</p>
<h2>Key factors that affect the service life of hydraulic hoses</h2>
<p>The degradation of the rubber and the internal reinforcement is in reality determined by several variables acting at the same time:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Working pressure and pressure peaks:</strong> continuous heavy loads and sudden hydraulic shocks (turbulence and pressure peaks) accelerate material fatigue.</li>
<li><strong>Ambient temperature and medium:</strong> extreme heat or cold, as well as the chemical composition and temperature of the fluid conveyed, attack the elastomer.</li>
<li><strong>Mechanical stress:</strong> friction against machine frames or a bend radius that is too tight cause premature wear of the cover.</li>
<li><strong>Environmental influences:</strong> UV radiation, ozone and aggressive substances (such as salt in the marine sector) accelerate the ageing of the rubber.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What do international standards say about the service life of hydraulic hoses?</h2>
<p>Leading international standards, such as the German DIN 20066, provide clear frameworks for the maximum service life of hose lines. In regular industrial applications, a total service life of no more than 6 years is recommended. This period consists of a maximum of 2 years of storage of the loose hose and a maximum of 4 years of operational use. Stricter, shorter periods may apply to specific sectors with increased safety risks or extreme loads.</p>
<p>{{img:kbi-35982-0}}</p>
<h2>How is it assessed whether a hose needs to be replaced?</h2>
<p>The actual condition of a hydraulic hose can only be determined by a targeted on-site inspection. This assessment is carried out through regular visual and functional checks, in which the entire hose line is carefully examined: from the flexible rubber cover to the critical transition to the hydraulic couplings.<br>
An important aid in this inspection is the so-called layline (the printed production line on the side of the hose). The layline contains vital information such as the manufacturer code, the applicable standard (for example EN 853 or EN 856) and the maximum working pressure.</p>
<p>In practice, there are five indicators that point directly to a dangerous weakening of the material.</p>
<h2>5 indicators that a hydraulic hose needs to be replaced:</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Visible steel reinforcement (damaged outer cover):</strong> when the rubber cover has worn away through abrasion or UV radiation to the point where the steel braid or spiral layers are visible, corrosion sets in quickly. This weakens the reinforcement.</li>
<li><strong>Leakage or "sweating" around the couplings:</strong> fluid seeping out between the rubber and the hydraulic hose couplings indicates an internal leak or a loosening crimp connection. According to the official maintenance guidelines of manufacturers such as Danfoss, "sweating" (minimal seepage of fluid through the cover) also indicates the beginning of structural material degradation and is the direct precursor of a hose failure.</li>
<li><strong>Kinking, twisting or exceeding the bend radius:</strong> a twisted or kinked hose leads to local narrowing, heat build-up and premature material fatigue. Installation instructions in product catalogues show that a hydraulic hose under pressure can expand by +2% and contract by -4%. A hose that was originally fitted without sufficient slack can become taut under pressure, which can lead to material fatigue.</li>
<li><strong>Blisters or soft spots on the cover:</strong> bubbles on the cover most likely indicate a tear in the inner tube. The oil is now pressing at high pressure directly against the thin outer layer, creating an immediate risk of bursting.</li>
<li><strong>Expired shelf life:</strong> the production date is always printed on the layline of the hose (usually shown as the quarter and year of manufacture, such as 2Q24 or *4Q25*). If the period in the DIN standard has been exceeded, or if the layline has become illegible due to external factors such as extreme wear or overspray, the hose should be renewed as a precaution.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Why is it incorrect to say that hydraulic hoses must be replaced after one or two years?</h2>
<p>Applying a standard replacement period of one or two years is incorrect, because the actual service life depends entirely on the specific application. A hose that operates indoors under constant, mild conditions can, for example, last many years longer than a hose that has to endure more. Replacing hoses as a rule after one or two years leads to unnecessary investments in such cases, provided that periodic, expert inspections take place.</p>
<h2>What goes wrong when replacing or repairing?</h2>
<p>As soon as an inspection shows that a hose needs to be replaced, action must be taken. It is precisely at that moment, when there is great time pressure to get a machine running again, that mistakes are regularly made that can jeopardise safety.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Emergency repairs to the hose:</strong> it is incorrect and dangerous to repair a damaged hose with tape, clamps or by welding it locally. The extreme forces in the system do not tolerate makeshift solutions.</li>
<li><strong>Combining different brands or product lines:</strong> combining hoses from manufacturer A with crimp couplings from manufacturer B is not recommended. In many cases this also applies within the product lines of a single manufacturer: the specific tolerances and crimp specs of, for example, a Danfoss Winner coupling differ from those of the Danfoss Aeroquip lines. Interchanging them can affect the structural integrity, so that the hydraulic hose cannot perform its function properly.</li>
<li><strong>Ignoring the cause:</strong> simply replacing a broken hose without addressing the underlying cause (such as a chafing machine frame or missing hydraulic hose protection) means the new hose will fail just as quickly.</li>
</ul>
<h2>How often should hydraulic hoses be inspected?</h2>
<p>The frequency of inspections depends on the operating conditions and the risk class of the installation. In heavy operational environments, such as the earthmoving or marine sector, a daily visual check before the work shift is recommended. For regular industrial installations, a thorough, documented inspection every 3 to 12 months is sufficient. Consistently keeping these inspection reports helps to detect hose wear at an early stage.</p>
<h2>Can hydraulic couplings be reused when replacing a hose?</h2>
<p>Reusing standard crimp couplings is incorrect and entails major safety risks. During the initial crimping, the metal ferrule undergoes permanent plastic deformation to clamp the hose watertight. Cutting off a used coupling and crimping it again onto a new hose affects the structural integrity of the metal, which under high pressure leads to an immediate risk of bursting or the coupling blowing off. It is therefore advised not to do this. Only specific screw couplings designed for this purpose (reusable fittings) may be reused under strict conditions and after thorough inspection.</p>
<h2>How is the length of a hydraulic hose measured correctly when replacing it?</h2>
<p>A common mistake when ordering a replacement hose is measuring only the flexible rubber part. International standards prescribe that the overall length (OAL) is decisive. This means that the hose is measured including the fitted couplings. The measurement is taken from the outermost sealing surface of one coupling to the outermost sealing surface of the other coupling. For elbow couplings, measurement is taken from the centre of the sealing surface. A correct OAL is crucial to guarantee the required slack when the system is pressurised.</p>
<h2>Timely maintenance ensures maximum hydraulic reliability</h2>
<p>Correctly assessing hydraulic components requires expertise and a systematic approach. By not relying blindly on fixed time intervals, but by steering on environmental factors, catalogue specifications, DIN standards and visual factors, an installation remains both safe and cost-effective.</p>
